WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.960
In this video, we’re going to give you a&nbsp;
step-by-step roadmap to ranking #1 in Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.720
in 2026, starting with the first lesson — don’t&nbsp;
ever trust anyone who tells you they can guarantee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.720
ranking #1 in Google. Sorry to disappoint, but&nbsp;
that’s just way beyond our control as marketers.&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:30.880
But, before you click off, what you can do is&nbsp;
create as many winning conditions as possible on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.680
your website to give you the highest-possible&nbsp;
chance of ranking highly in Google.&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.160
That’s a less sexy, but&nbsp;
more accurate way to say it.&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.618
My name is Matt and I run a consultancy called&nbsp;
Kenyon Digital where I have helped clients hit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.618 --> 00:00:46.791
#1 spot for their key terms and have come up with&nbsp;
a repeatable process that can help just about any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.791 --> 00:00:55.280
business or brand get there. I’ve partnered with&nbsp;
Surfer to give you that playbook in this video.&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.040
So before we get into the nitty-gritty, we have&nbsp;
to take realistic stock of what we’re after. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.560
going to give you three reality checks here.
First, If you are looking to rank #1 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.320
ultra-competitive terms — terms that have been&nbsp;
dominated by enterprise-level businesses for years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.960
or decades, that’s probably not a feasible task.
These businesses have spent years building their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.760
authority and millions of dollars on&nbsp;
their content. It’s not impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:30.000
but it’s probably not worth the resources,&nbsp;
especially for smaller to medium-sized businesses.&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.480
Secondly, you have to understand that it’s one&nbsp;
thing to rank #1 and it’s another thing to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.800
#1. Once you crack that top spot for a particular&nbsp;
keyword, especially a competitive one, you’ve got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.320
a target on your back, and all your competitors&nbsp;
will be trying outdo you in content quality and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.000
build more links than you. So, just know that&nbsp;
getting the ranking is half the battle, keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.800
it is an ongoing effort that requires regular&nbsp;
content refreshing, authority-building, and more.&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.720
And thirdly, you don’t need to rank #1 for&nbsp;
every keyword relevant to your business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.680
in fact it may be a waste of resources&nbsp;
to try to do so. For any given business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.960
there are likely 1,000s of potential keywords&nbsp;
across their service offerings and content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:23.360
and expecting or trying to rank all of them #1&nbsp;
is just not realistic (nor is it necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:30.240
have a very successful organic search presence).
One of the hardest things about SEO is not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.640
execution, it’s the prioritization, and that’s&nbsp;
something I hope to help you with in this video.&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.031
Okay, so our minds are calibrated, our&nbsp;
expectations are set… let’s get ranking.&nbsp;

00:02:42.031 --> 00:02:44.080
Step #1 - Identifying Your Most Valuable Keywords
Now, I know many of you watching this already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.160
have a website, likely with some ranking keywords,&nbsp;
whether they’re ranking high or low is a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.600
story — but I just want you to forget about&nbsp;
that for a second and start with a clean slate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:02.400
Ask yourself — what are the business-driving&nbsp;
keywords that in a perfect world, my prospects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.440
would type into Google and then find me.
This is going to be different for different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.160
types of businesses. But the first is almost&nbsp;
always going to be your brand name. The next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.800
most important are likely going to be&nbsp;
explicit names of products or services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:23.040
you sell or keywords describing those things.
Warby Parker, a direct to consumer eyewear brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.840
targets keywords like:
"buy glasses online"&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.000
"affordable prescription glasses"
"home try-on glasses"&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.440
DocuSign (B2B SaaS Company),&nbsp;
will target keywords like:&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.800
“electronic signature software”
“sign documents online”&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.200
or “DocuSign alternatives” (yes,&nbsp;
you want to rank for this, too)&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.800
And for Nicks Plumbing, a local business in&nbsp;
Houston, TX, target keywords might include:&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.800
“plumber near me”
“Houston plumber”&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.040
or names of specific services like:
“drain cleaning Houston”&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.040
“water heater repair Houston”
or “sewer line repair Houston”&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.120
Once you’ve identified your core keywords,&nbsp;
Google each of them and really study the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.160
results page. The first thing you’ll notice — if&nbsp;
you haven’t noticed already — is that in 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.200
the search engine results pages (or SERPs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.000
short) look very different than they&nbsp;
did even just a few short years ago.&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.600
For example, when I Google “electronic signature&nbsp;
software,” I see a series of sponsored posts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.960
a Reddit discussion as the top organic result,&nbsp;
a listicle of the best electronic signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.480
software platforms, and some homepages of&nbsp;
popular software companies, like Docusign.&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:39.040
Depending on how Google is feeling that day, you&nbsp;
may also see YouTube video results, AI Overviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:46.400
or Accordion-style People Also Ask questions. So&nbsp;
yeah, we’ve come a long way from 10 blue links.&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.280
It’s easy to get overwhelmed here, but&nbsp;
our main goal is to figure out one thing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:58.480
what is the search intent behind this keyword —&nbsp;
or put another way, what do you think people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:04.880
trying to find when they search this? And perhaps&nbsp;
more importantly, what does Google think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.720
are trying to find when they search this? I&nbsp;
call this Google’s Asssumed Search Intent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:17.280
and it’s very important that your content meets&nbsp;
that search intent as closely as possible.&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.160
Now, I’m glad I used this particular query&nbsp;
(”electronic signature software”) as an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.000
because it has mixed-intent, meaning&nbsp;
there are multiple possible intents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.280
that people can have when searching this.&nbsp;
How do we know this? We look at the SERP.&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.360
Someone searching for this term might want to&nbsp;
eavesdrop on some Reddit discussions about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.160
best software for e-signatures. Or, they might&nbsp;
want to read a round-up post comparing a list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.600
the best platforms. Or, maybe they just want&nbsp;
to be taken straight to a vendor’s homepage.&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:54.880
Google, despite its sophistication, doesn’t quite&nbsp;
know, so it gives the user a all-you-can-click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.960
buffet of options and lets them decide.
But this poses a problem, because I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.480
said the most important thing is matching your&nbsp;
page’s content to the keyword’s search intent. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.120
how do you do that when search intent is mixed?
There are a few different approaches you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.440
take. I’ll explain them and then&nbsp;
share which one I like the best.&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.760
The first is simply adopting the majority view.&nbsp;
If 6 out of 10 results are homepages. You stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.960
with targeting that keyword on your homepage.
The second is more strategic, and it involves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.920
asking what the most likely approach would be&nbsp;
to get you featured on the SERP based on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.240
brand’s authority and existing search&nbsp;
presence. This is the approach that Get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.480
Accept has taken with their round-up post.
See, DocuSign and PandaDoc have massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:07:00.240
brand recognition, which allows them to rank their&nbsp;
homepage for this keyword. GetAccept doesn’t yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:06.320
so they kind of find a backdoor approach. Rather&nbsp;
than attempting to go band-for-band on homepages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.200
they say, “We’ll just publish a round-up&nbsp;
page giving our users a ton of free value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.040
even to the point of mentioning our&nbsp;
competitors, and let them decide.”&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:20.560
And even though they don’t have the site authority&nbsp;
of PandaDoc or DocuSign, they still manage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.800
outrank them with their round-up post.
(Just another reason why round-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:25.805
posts are awesome.)
Step #2 - Build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.805 --> 00:07:27.600
Best Possible Page for That Keyword
Okay, so you've identified your target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:35.360
keyword and studied the SERP. You know what Google&nbsp;
thinks users want. Step #2 is to build the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.800
possible page for that keyword.
Let's break this down into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.480
the components that actually matter.
Firstly, matching the format to the intent.&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.520
Webpages come in all different shapes and&nbsp;
sizes. A product page looks and feels quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.240
different from a long-form informational&nbsp;
blog post, which likewise looks different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:00.080
than an eCommerce category page.
Your job is to match that format of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:06.800
top-ranking results for your target keyword, while&nbsp;
differentiating the substance of that content.&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.320
(And yes, if the format is a video,&nbsp;
which is very common in how-to Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:16.080
searches, you need to be making video!)
And real quick, I don’t know how many more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:22.560
times we need to keep saying this, but Google does&nbsp;
not prefer or reward a particular word count. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:28.320
question to ask isn't "how long should this be?"&nbsp;
It's "what does someone searching this term need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.520
to know, and have I covered it completely?"
For every piece of content I create,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:38.240
I start by adding my target keyword into&nbsp;
Surfer’s Content Editor. For this piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.600
I’m targeting “how to start a hydroponic garden.”
I love Surfer’s new step-by-step process here in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.320
the right-hand panel, which takes the guesswork&nbsp;
out of each step of the writing workflow.&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.480
I’ll usually start by looking through the&nbsp;
competitors’ outlines to get my creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.920
juices flowing and make sure I’m covering this&nbsp;
topic at least as thoroughly as my competitors.&nbsp;

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.400
You can also create an AI-generated outline,&nbsp;
which you don’t need to use in its entirety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:09.600
but can be great for giving you some ideas&nbsp;
– and it’s all based on real data from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.480
top-ranking pages in the SERP.
As part of my research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.680
I also click the Questions tab to make sure&nbsp;
I’m answering all of these in the content.&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:23.200
After I’ve finished a draft in my own words, I’ll&nbsp;
retroactively go back and make sure I’m including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:29.200
all the entities I need to optimize for both&nbsp;
traditional search engines and AI assistants.&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.680
By the way, I think we’re long overdue for&nbsp;
a full content workflow from start to finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.160
using Surfer. I don’t have enough time to deep&nbsp;
dive into that right now, but leave a comment if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.800
that’s something you’d like to see soon.
Now, just having a well-optimized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:49.680
thorough piece of content isn’t enough. The second&nbsp;
phase of creating great content that ranks highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:56.720
is demonstrating real expertise and experience.
You may have heard the acronym EEAT thrown around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:02.480
that’s a Google term that stands for Experience,&nbsp;
Expertise, Authoritativeness and Trustworthiness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:09.120
and it’s how Google claims to evaluate content.
Google has gotten very good at identifying whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.320
content comes from first-hand experience&nbsp;
or was just assembled from other sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:19.200
According to some reputable SEO sources,&nbsp;
the extra "E" — Experience — is now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.400
most heavily weighted factor.
What signals real experience?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:30.400
Original photos. Screenshots. Step-by-step&nbsp;
documentation from actually doing the thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.680
Case studies with specific numbers. Personal&nbsp;
anecdotes about what worked and what didn't.&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:41.760
It just so happens that the GetAccept round-up&nbsp;
post we referenced earlier does this very well.&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.840
First, they explicitly establish&nbsp;
authority with this sub-header "Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.560
we really have tested each platform."
Second, they include original screenshots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:51.530
every single platform. These are presumably not&nbsp;
stock images or marketing graphics pulled from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:53.360
company websites, but actual UI screenshots from&nbsp;
inside the products. You can see document editors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.160
signature flows, dashboards. These are the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.400
images you can only get by actually&nbsp;
logging in and using the software.&nbsp;

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.960
Third, they structure each review&nbsp;
consistently: a "Best for" badge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:10.880
a description, a screenshot, pros, cons,&nbsp;
and an ideal use case. That consistency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:16.960
shows methodology. It shows they ran each&nbsp;
platform through the same evaluation framework.&nbsp;

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:22.400
And finally, there's a real author with a&nbsp;
byline and photo. Natasha Ellis-Knight. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:28.000
can see when it was published and when it&nbsp;
was last updated. These are small details,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.000
but they signal accountability.&nbsp;
Someone's name is attached to this.&nbsp;

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.920
In addition to all these experience signals, they&nbsp;
have a nice table of contents, and a tl;dr section&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.680
making the piece highly convenient for skimming.
So — If you're writing about a product, have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:49.280
actually used it? If you're explaining a process,&nbsp;
have you actually done it? If you're reviewing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:54.480
service, have you actually hired them?
If the answer is no, either get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:59.200
experience or find someone who has&nbsp;
it and involve them in the content.&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:03.360
I mean heck this even be someone you&nbsp;
find in the reviews of the products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.080
that you interview for their experience. You&nbsp;
don’t need to be an expert in everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:14.000
you just need to demonstrate expertise and&nbsp;
experience, even if it’s from someone else.&nbsp;

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.040
Okay, so you’ve matched format to intent,&nbsp;
you’ve built a great page that satisfies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.960
EEAT signals and… you’re good to go! Be sure&nbsp;
to like and subscribe for more tips and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:31.200
fun making all that money! No, of course not,&nbsp;
if SEO were that easy, brand new websites could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.720
outrank behemoth brands in a matter of days.
No, unfortunately, building an amazing page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:42.000
isn’t nearly enough, especially if you’re a newer&nbsp;
site or one that’s typically gotten lower search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:49.811
traffic. You have to power up your pages,&nbsp;
and that’s step #3 in ranking #1 in Google.&nbsp;

00:12:49.811 --> 00:12:52.000
Step #3 - Power Up Your Pages
Okay, so how do we power up our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:58.480
money pages such that Google and users trust&nbsp;
us with their clicks? The main ways are:&nbsp;

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:02.640
By building topical authority around&nbsp;
your target keyword through helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:08.560
interrelated content pieces all connected by&nbsp;
internal links (I’ll break that down in a second)&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:14.800
By acquiring healthy, natural backlinks&nbsp;
from other websites to your website&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.320
Let’s keep it real simple — pretend you’re&nbsp;
Google and you are tasked with evaluating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:26.640
whether or not two different websites should rank&nbsp;
for the keyword, “best hydroponic garden system.”&nbsp;

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:31.920
Both websites have a great page on the topic with&nbsp;
expert advice, engaging on-page elements like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:39.120
infographics, and plenty of firsthand experience.
But website A only has one page on the overarching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:45.280
topic of hydroponic gardening, freshly published&nbsp;
a couple weeks ago. Website B on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:51.840
has an entire ecosystem of related content&nbsp;
pieces covering hydroponic gardening from every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:57.520
conceivable angle. Best hydroponic gardens for&nbsp;
vegetables, for outdoor growing, for apartments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:02.080
for limited space, best gardens on a&nbsp;
budget — you name it, they have it.&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:07.200
Not only that but since they’ve developed so much&nbsp;
great content, they’ve earned powerful backlinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:12.720
from sites like Better Homes and Gardens&nbsp;
and a bunch of niche gardening websites.&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:18.080
Which would you choose to rank for “best&nbsp;
hydroponic garden system”? — keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:23.680
no difference in quality on the page itself,&nbsp;
but the answer is obvious. We’d choose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:29.920
one with proven trust from other websites&nbsp;
and clear topical authority on the subject.&nbsp;

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:35.040
This is exactly why Surfer exists and&nbsp;
why I personally use it and have used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.000
even before making videos on this channel.
Surfer's Sites dashboard takes the guesswork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.360
out of building topical authority. You plug in&nbsp;
your domain, connect it to Google Search Console,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.840
and Surfer analyzes your existing&nbsp;
content coverage — showing you exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.980
where you stand and where the gaps are.
It clusters related keywords together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:52.049
visualizes your topical coverage,&nbsp;
so you can see at a glance: "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.049 --> 00:14:52.135
I've covered hydroponic gardens for beginners&nbsp;
pretty well, but I'm completely missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.135 --> 00:14:52.228
content around hydroponic lighting, nutrient&nbsp;
solutions, and troubleshooting common problems."&nbsp;

00:14:52.228 --> 00:14:56.000
Those gaps? Those are your next content&nbsp;
priorities. Each piece you publish fills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:00.160
in more of that topical map, signals to&nbsp;
Google that you actually know what you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:05.040
talking about, and creates more opportunities&nbsp;
for internal linking back to your money pages.&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.480
And once you've identified what to&nbsp;
write, Surfer's Content Editor helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:14.320
you build each piece with the right semantic&nbsp;
keywords, proper structure, and comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:20.720
factual coverage from both traditional and AI&nbsp;
Search platforms — so every supporting article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:26.800
actually moves the needle on your authority.
The result is a content ecosystem where every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:31.840
piece supports every other piece, and&nbsp;
your money pages sit at the center of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:38.400
a web of trust signals pointing right at them.
Now, let’s talk about the second portion of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.160
“powering up” equation: getting backlinks&nbsp;
from other websites to your website.&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:49.520
Backlinking is a massive topic that deserves&nbsp;
its own video or even series of videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:54.400
so as much as I’m tempted, I can’t go down the&nbsp;
rabbit hole today. That said, I do have a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:59.520
on my own channel, Matt Kenyon Marketing, of&nbsp;
my favorite backlink acquisition techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:04.000
which you can check out via the info card or&nbsp;
link in the description if you’re interested.&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.960
I will say this though — and I will die&nbsp;
on this hill — I believe it’s far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:15.840
important to build your topical authority with&nbsp;
interrelated pieces of content first before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:20.720
you go hunting for backlinks, and here’s why.
Let’s stick with the hydroponics thing — say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:25.280
you’re trying to build a hydroponics supply&nbsp;
brand and you start doing some outreach to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:30.880
websites for backlinks. Of course, my preference&nbsp;
would just be to create content that’s so good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:35.280
that other sites link to you naturally, but&nbsp;
everyone gets very upset when you say that.&nbsp;

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:40.480
Anyway — which would be an easier sell to try to&nbsp;
get a backlink from a site owner? Would it be:&nbsp;

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:45.280
“Hey, can you please link to my&nbsp;
sales page because I want money” OR&nbsp;

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:49.200
“Hey, I noticed that you linked to a page&nbsp;
on an old site that hasn’t been updated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:54.480
in 6 years on the best hydroponic gardening&nbsp;
nutrient solutions. I just wrote a brand new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:59.360
post where I tested a bunch and I think it could&nbsp;
really help your audience, plus, transparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:04.320
I would honestly love a backlink from your site.”
It’s a no-brainer — they’d almost be silly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:08.240
not to link to you given the value&nbsp;
you’re providing to their audience.&nbsp;

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:13.600
So great, you get the backlink to your blog post,&nbsp;
how does that power up your money pages though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:21.120
how does that help make moneyyyyyy my dude.
Two words. Some of the sweetest words in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:29.280
of SEO. “Internal linking.” More specifically&nbsp;
“Internal linking passing link authority to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:36.720
pages on your site.” That’s 11 words and doesn’t&nbsp;
quite roll off the tongue, but here’s the point:&nbsp;

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:41.600
When you build content clusters around your&nbsp;
money pages, and then internally link everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:47.760
together, all the backlink authority that each of&nbsp;
those supporting pages get (like our hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:56.000
blog post on nutrient solutions), gets passed up&nbsp;
to the money page through those internal links,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.680
boosting the authority of the&nbsp;
money page in search results.&nbsp;

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:05.600
Then, you rinse and repeat, applying this process&nbsp;
to each and every one of your revenue-driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:12.400
pages, and the rising tides of organic&nbsp;
growth lifts the entire ship of your website.&nbsp;

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:17.440
Are you seeing the power&nbsp;
of this? Isn’t SEO insane?!&nbsp;

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:22.240
(I didn’t think as a little child who wanted to&nbsp;
make Pixar movies that I’d be saying that about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:28.240
a digital marketing strategy but here we are.)
Now this is a lot of hard work — I just described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:32.800
doing this for one particular money page.&nbsp;
If you have multiple products or services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:37.920
you’ll be rinsing and repeating for each of them.
I’m condensing this process for the sake of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:41.600
video, but creating just one of these&nbsp;
topical clusters around your money page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:46.000
and building links to those pages can&nbsp;
take several months of full-time work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:50.480
and even then there’s no guarantee that you’ll&nbsp;
see results for another few more months.&nbsp;

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:53.920
And also I’m assuming that you&nbsp;
already have good product-market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.920 --> 00:19:00.160
fit and a technically sound website.
But SEO rewards the patient. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:05.680
traffic gained from SEO is high-intent&nbsp;
and high-quality, and you can often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:11.280
reap the rewards of it for years to come.
When you study the patterns of the #1 ranking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:17.600
websites, no matter what industry, they follow&nbsp;
this playbook. They build killer money pages with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:24.080
compelling copy and engaging visuals, they create&nbsp;
whole ecosystems of content around those pages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:30.800
and build authority and trust from the rest of the&nbsp;
internet, sometimes over the course of decades.&nbsp;

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:38.000
So that’s the move — it’s not easy, but it’s very&nbsp;
doable, even for small to medium-sized businesses.&nbsp;

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:43.280
And the best part is that you don't have to do&nbsp;
it alone and you don't have to do it manually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

